{
 "issues": [
  {
   "analyst": "S1",
   "problem": "The delay calculation constructs both scheduled and actual timestamps on the scheduled UTC date but never adjusts an actual departure occurring after midnight to the following day. This can turn an overnight delayed departure into a large negative delay and undercount delayed flights. This explains the 9/24 bad-weather numerator versus the 10/24 numerator reported by S2/S3."
  },
  {
   "analyst": "S3",
   "problem": "The rollover rule adds 1,440 minutes whenever the clock difference is less than -60 minutes. The documentation explicitly allows early departures, so an actual departure more than an hour early could be incorrectly treated as occurring the next day. A threshold of -60 is not justified by the data definition; S2's much more conservative -720 threshold is better supported, though any rollover rule should be documented and checked against the affected records."
  }
 ],
 "disagreements": "S1 reports bad_weather_rate 37.5% (9/24), while S2 and S3 report 41.7% (10/24). They agree on other_rate = 6.8% and appear to agree on the weather classification, local scheduled-date/hour matching, cancellation exclusion, and the strict >15-minute delay definition. The bad-weather disagreement is attributable to overnight actual departures: S1 fails to adjust them, whereas S2 and S3 do. S2 and S3's matching results do not by themselves establish that S3's -60-minute rollover threshold is safe, because early departures are permitted.",
 "best": "S2",
 "correction": "Use S2's result: {\"bad_weather_rate\": 41.7, \"other_rate\": 6.8}. The calculation must account for completed flights whose actual departure is after midnight; retain the documented bad-weather rule and exclude cancellations from both rates. S2 is preferable to S3 because its -720-minute rollover cutoff is less likely to reinterpret legitimate early departures as next-day departures."
}
